// D-04-03's decisive research witness (04-CONTEXT.md), converted to
// A-normal form per D-07-44: the recorded witness's `escort` passes a
// NESTED expression as a call argument --
//
//   let aliased = relay(borrow mut buffer)
//
// -- which D-07-01 has just made ungrammatical: a call's single argument
// must be the name of an in-scope binding, never a `borrow`/`borrow mut`/
// `take`/literal/nested-call expression. The conversion binds the
// exclusive borrow first, THEN passes that binding to the callee, then
// moves the owner -- exactly the "bind first, pass the binding, then move"
// rewrite D-07-44 prescribes:
//
//   let borrowed = borrow mut buffer   // was the inline `borrow mut buffer`
//   let aliased = relay(borrowed)      // was `relay(borrow mut buffer)`
//   let delivered = take buffer        // unchanged
//   aliased                            // unchanged
//
// Equivalence argument: `borrowed` names exactly the same exclusive-borrow
// operation the original inline expression denoted, evaluated at exactly
// the same program point (immediately before the call, with no
// intervening operation that could observe or invalidate it). Naming an
// expression before using it once, with nothing else scheduled in
// between, cannot change what is aliased, when the alias is created, or
// when `buffer`'s ownership is later moved -- the two programs describe
// the identical sequence of operations under different surface syntax.
// `relay` itself is unchanged: byte-for-byte the `exclusive_borrow_clean`
// shape 04-CONTEXT.md names (also `testdata/phase07/
// clean_but_unpublishable.lang`'s own `relay`, restated here in the
// `phase07.relay_escort_witness` module namespace since calls resolve only
// within a single parsed program, D-07-01).
//
// D-07-44 (finding, not failure): this witness checks CLEAN under Phase
// 07's rules. `check`'s own loan-liveness law (computeLoanLastUses) builds
// its shadow use-chain from each binding's RHS.Source; a "call" binding
// carries RHS.Arguments, not RHS.Source, so the call at
// `let aliased = relay(borrowed)` is invisible to that chain as a use of
// `borrowed` -- the exclusive loan on `buffer` is treated as ending at its
// own creation point, with no visible use surviving into or past the call.
// `take buffer` on the next line therefore sees no active loan and is
// admitted, even though `aliased` (the call's own return, standing in for
// the borrowed view `relay` actually returns) remains live afterward. This
// is exactly the INTERPROCEDURAL half of D-03-02 -- calls do not yet
// propagate loan liveness across the function boundary -- and it stays
// open until Phase 08/09's interprocedural loan-liveness work closes it
// (D-03-02, D-05-32/D-05-33). The clean outcome is asserted explicitly
// below by `TestRelayEscortWitnessChecksCleanPendingInterproceduralLiveness`
// (check_test.go) precisely so a future phase's change to this behavior is
// visible, never silently re-broken or silently re-fixed.
//
// The pre-existing `fn relay` in testdata/phase3/shared_shared_accept.lang
// (module owned.shared_shared_accept) is a DIFFERENT fixture: an owned
// take/return with two overlapping SHARED loans, no exclusive borrow, no
// dangling-alias shape, and it publishes with no problems at all. Do not
// confuse the two.

module phase07.relay_escort_witness

export {
  fn escort
}

fn relay(buffer: Buffer) -> borrow mut(buffer) Buffer {
  let view = borrow mut buffer
  view
}

fn escort(buffer: Buffer) -> borrow mut(buffer) Buffer {
  let borrowed = borrow mut buffer
  let aliased = relay(borrowed)
  let delivered = take buffer
  aliased
}
